<Byteblazar>
    <append xpath="/blocks">
        <block name="SingularityConfig">
            <property name="Extends" value="terrDesertGround"/>

            <property class="XMLExtensions">

				<!--
					Automatic Changes:
					==================

					Singularity adds the following new entity classes for use in AITask/AITarget properties:

						EntityAnimalChicken (inherits from EntityAnimalRabbit)

						EntityAnimalBear (inherits from EntityEnemyAnimal)
						EntityAnimalBoar (inherits from EntityEnemyAnimal)
						EntityAnimalMountainLion (inherits from EntityEnemyAnimal)

						EntityAnimalWolf (inherits from EntityEnemyAnimal)
							EntityAnimalCoyote (inherits from EntityAnimalWolf)

						EntityAnimalSupernatural (inherits from EntityEnemyAnimal)
							EntityAnimalBossGrace (inherits from EntityAnimalSupernatural)
							EntityAnimalDireWolf (inherits from EntityAnimalSupernatural)
							EntityAnimalZombieBear (inherits from EntityAnimalSupernatural)

						EntityZombieSmart (inherits from EntityZombie)
							EntityZombieScreamer (inherits from EntityZombieSmart)

					Note that these have no effect unless first declared in entity_classes as "Class" properties:

						<entity_class name="Platypus"/>
							<property name="Class" value="EntityAnimalChicken"/>

					As of 1.6.0, Singularity adds the following operations when using <set> or <setattribute> to modify numeric attributes:

						addInt(addend)
						addFloat(addend)
						subtractInt(subtrahend)
						subtractFloat(subtrahend)
						multiplyInt(factor)
						multiplyFloat(factor)
						divideInt(divisor)
						divideFloat(divisor)
						exponentiateInt(exponent)
						exponentiateFloat(exponent)
						clampInt(min,max)
						clampFloat(min,max)

						Syntax & Usage:
							<set xpath="xpath/@attributeNameHere" singularity_op="operation(terms)">placeholder_value</set>
							<setattribute xpath="xpath/here" name="attributeNameHere" singularity_op="operation(terms)">placeholder_value</setattribute>

						Example:
							Let's say we want all melee tools to deal 50% more block damage. We can simply do:						
							<set xpath="/items/item[starts-with(@name,'meleeTool')]/effect_group/passive_effect[@name='BlockDamage']/@value" singularity_op="multiplyInt(1.5)">30</set>
										
						Notes:
							- These operations are performed on the existing attribute values. When the operation succeeds, the placeholder_value is fully ignored.
							- When the operation cannot take place because the existing attribute value is not numeric, the existing value remains intact.
							- When using <set>, the placeholder_value is applied only if Singularity is not installed, which avoids parsing errors/warnings when loading.
							- When using <setattribute>, if the target attribute does not exist, no operation is performed. The attribute is created with the placeholder_value instead. This matches the original behavior of <setattribute> and ensures that missing attributes are added normally, without requiring a numeric value to operate on.
							- All of these operations take floating point values as arguments, even the ones ending in "int" like "multiplyInt." The difference with the ones ending in "float" is that the former round the result to an integer.
				-->
				
                <property name="ShaderlessFX" value="true"/>
				 <!-- 
					Adds two new screen effects: Singularity_Snapshot and Singularity_Overlay. They can be used via XML as ModifyScreenEffect triggered actions. Singularity_Snapshot takes a snapshot of the whole screen and applies it as an overlay. 
				 
						Example:
						<triggered_effect trigger="onSelfBuffStart" action="ModifyScreenEffect" fade="1" effect_name="Singularity_Snapshot"/>
				 
					Singularity_Overlay applies a uniform color overlay. By default the overlay will be white, but you can optionally declare a "color" attribute (rgba).
				 
						Examples:
						<triggered_effect trigger="onSelfBuffStart" action="ModifyScreenEffect" intensity="1" fade=".8" effect_name="Singularity_Overlay"/> 
						<triggered_effect trigger="onSelfBuffStart" action="ModifyScreenEffect" intensity=".5" fade="1" effect_name="Singularity_Overlay" color="FF0000"/> 
				 -->
                <property name="EntityStun" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					Any entity with a "Singularity_Stunned" CVar value greater than 0 will stop chasing and looking at other entities. They will still get alerted and even attack enemies within their reach, but they will not move from their position or turn to face them outside of that range. For compatibility between mods, it's recommended to use add and subtract operations instead of set (add 1 to initiate the stun, subtract 1 when done). 
				-->
                <property name="NonlethalExplosives" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					Adds a new "Singularity_IsNonlethalExplosive" property that can be added to explosives via XML to prevent them from dealing damage but still apply whatever buffs they have set in "Explosion.Buffs" to all targets within their blast radius (not ignoring cover). 
				-->
                <property name="AmmoSounds" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					Adds two new properties for ammo: "Singularity_Sound_start" and "Singularity_Sound_loop" that override the respective weapon sounds when using that ammo.
				-->
                <property name="EntityGregariousness" value="true"/>
				<!-- 
					Any entity_class with a Singularity_Gregariousness property will have a chance of assisting other entities of its class that are getting attacked nearby (normalized percentage from 0.0 to 1.0). Example with 50% chance:
					
					<append xpath="/entity_classes/entity_class[@name='animalBoar']">
						<property name="Singularity_Gregariousness" value="0.5"/>
					
					As of 1.2.0.0, this property also causes entities to more formally band together around a leader. Every time members wander too far away from their leaders, they eventually return (unless they're unable to do so for any reason, like dying).
					
					As of 1.3.0.0, the maximum size of each group of entities can also be specified in the following way (example with a max group size of 4):
					
					<append xpath="/entity_classes/entity_class[@name='animalBoar']">
						<property name="Singularity_GroupSize" value="4"/>
					
					This number represents slots for each leader of a group (counting the leader). Note that once group members die, their slots are NOT freed. This is on purpose, as it makes it more likely for multiple groups of different sizes (up to max) to exist simultaneously, instead of fewer large groups.
					
					Defaults to -1 (no group size limit).
				-->
                <property name="SetAsTargetIfHurt_BypassSameTypeCheck" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					This feature removes the requirement from SetAsTargetIfHurt AITarget tasks for entities to be of different entityType (enum).
				-->
                <property name="RunawayFromEntity_NoImplicitPlayer" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					This feature makes it so RunawayFromEntity doesn't automatically cause entities to run away from players. Players must then be declared explicitly in XML when that behavior is desired.
				-->
                <property name="SetNearestCorpseAsTarget_Filters" value="false"/>
				<!-- 
					Extends SetNearestCorpseAsTarget in the following ways:
					- Adds a class parameter to filter by entity class (much like SetNearestEntityAsTarget, but simpler syntax)
					- Adds a cannibalismChance parameter that gives each entity a chance of eating the remains of members of its own class (normalized percentage from 0.0 to 1.0). Defaults to 0.0 if not set (no cannibalism)
					
					Example:
					<property name="AITarget-1" value="SetNearestCorpseAsTarget" data="class=EntityAnimalRabbit,EntityAnimalBoar;cannibalismChance=0.20"/>
						
					The above example translates to: Entity will eat dead rabbits and boars nearby. If the entity itself is a rabbit or a boar, there will be a 20% chance it will be flagged as a cannibal upon spawning, and if so, it will eat the remains of other rabbits or boars, otherwise it won't.
				-->
            </property>

            <property class="ScreenEffects">
                <property name="DisableBloodDropsOverlay" value="false"/>
				<!-- Completely disables the blood drops overlay that occurs when players take damage from any source. -->
                <property name="UseCustomDrowningEffects" value="true"/>
				<!-- Replaces the blood drop overlay with custom effects when drowning. Requires ShaderlessFX. -->
                <property name="UseCustomBurningEffects" value="true"/>
				<!-- Replaces the blood drop overlay with custom effects when burning. Requires ShaderlessFX. -->
            </property>
			
			<property class="ServerPolicies">
				<property name="EnforceBrightness" value="false"/>
				<property name="TargetBrightness" value="0.5" />
				<!-- Forces clients to use the chosen Brightness (OptionsGfxBrightness). Valid values: from 0.0 (0%) to 1.0 (100%) -->
				<property name="NoRespawnNearBackpack" value="false" />
				<!-- Disables the option to spawn near the backpack and overrides the behavior of the Respawn Now button to ensure the player spawns in one of the world's starting spawn points. -->
			</property>
			
			<property class="AdvancedSkyManager">
				<property name="Enabled" value="false"/>
				<!-- Master switch for all SkyManager features. If false, none of the below settings apply. -->
				<property name="DawnHour" value="5" />
				<property name="DuskHour" value="20" />
				<!-- Dawn and dusk start times in 24-hour format (0–23). Singularity overrides the server's DayLightLength setting, deriving it from these values. Daytime and nighttime are also derived from these, so they actually affect all systems tied to them, meaning zombies still switch to day/night movement speeds at the expected times, night biome spawns keep occurring at night, etc. -->
				<property name="BloodmoonVisibleDawnOffset" value="0" />
				<property name="BloodmoonVisibleDuskOffset" value="-4" />
				<!-- Dawn and dusk offsets for Blood Moon effects. Vanilla uses 2 and -4, respectively, so 2 hours after dawn and 4 hours before dusk. -->
				<property name="TraderOpenTime" value="5:05" />
				<property name="TraderCloseTime" value="19:50" />
				<!-- Shifts trader opening/closing times to match the chosen schedule. In vanilla, they open 5 minutes after dawn and close 10 minutes before dusk, so this defaults to that. Traders set to be open 24/7 are unaffected. -->
				<property name="RealisticNoon" value="true"/>
				<!-- Automatically adjusts DawnHour/DuskHour if it's necessary to ensure solar noon falls between 11:00 and 13:00. If your values are way off, dawn and/or dusk won't happen at the exact times you set them to. Set this to false if you really do want to use absurd values (like dawn at 5am with dusk at 10am). -->
				<property name="RealisticSunPath" value="true"/>
				<!-- Replaces the vanilla linear sun movement with a realistic path based on astronomy. The sun's altitude follows a sine-like curve, and its azimuth varies throughout the day. Takes into account axial tilt, latitude, and the season (derived from day length). -->
				<property name="Latitude" value="41" />
				<!-- Your latitude in degrees, from –70 (southern hemisphere) to 70 (northern hemisphere). Used only when RealisticSunPath is enabled. Most 7DTD players probably live somewhere in the 30-60° range. -->
				<!-- DEPRECATED: <property name="AdjustTraderHours" value="true" /> -->
				<property name="AdjustRestorePowerQuestStartTimes" value="true" />
				<!-- The time window to start Restore Power quests begins at dusk and ends halfway through the night, mirroring vanilla behavior. If you set this to false, the static vanilla values are used instead (22:00 to 01:00), so it will most likely not match your new nighttime period as well as you would expect. -->
			</property>

        </block>
    </append>
</Byteblazar>